moves the manual heap lifetime pair entirely and turns the buffer into owned Rust storage. Per C12, III② wins over eager-zero-init shrinkage for this buffer: once the buffer is a Rust container, hit [0] becomes moot rather than something to stack separately. This also gives a cleaner base for any in-body alias/local rewrites."}, {"rule": "C3", "strategy": "Apply S2-style local hoisting for loop-invariant raw-pointer reads/derived values inside the decode loop, especially invariant callback/user-data/function-pointer loads and input-size pointer dereferences, without changing the public signature.", "target_hits": [1, 2, 3, 4], "needs_cross_fn": false, "rationale": "After III②, the remaining loop still uses raw pointers and an indirect callback path, so hoisting invariant loads into locals is the lowest-risk way to recover GVN/LICM opportunities. I will not use S1/signature lifting here because that would cascade into caller changes and overlaps with III① ambitions. Doing C3 after III② lets the hoisted locals refer to stable owned-buffer bases instead of malloc results."}, {"rule": "C6", "strategy": "Hoist loop-invariant dispatch/selection around the callback path so the loop does not repeatedly re-resolve invariant behavior each iteration; keep the general fallback semantics intact.", "target_hits": [9], "needs_cross_fn": false, "rationale": "The callback function pointer and surrounding branch behavior are invariant across the loop. Applying C6 after C3 is synergistic: C3 first identifies/cacheable invariant state, then C6 can structure the loop around those cached invariants. This remains intra-function and avoids the larger cross-function generic rewrite of III①."}], "abstained_rules": [{"rule": "C12", "reason": "Subsumed by III② on hit [0]: the oversized eager zero-init is the heap dictionary buffer, and replacing manual malloc/free with an owned Rust buffer removes the original eager-zero-init/memset shape from consideration more cleanly than a separate MaybeUn